      "text": "To solve for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) given that \\(g(f(x)) = 3x + 4\\) for all \\(x\\), we start by substituting the expressions for \\(f(x)\\) and \\(g(x)\\) into \\(g(f(x))\\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[ f(x) = ax + b \\]\n\\[ g(x) = 2x - 5 \\]\n\\[ g(f(x)) = 3x + 4 \\]\n\nFirst, we compute \\(g(f(x))\\):\n\\[ g(f(x)) = g(ax + b) \\]\nSince \\(g(x) = 2x - 5\\), we substitute \\(ax + b\\) for \\(x\\) in \\(g(x)\\):\n\\[ g(ax + b) = 2(ax + b) - 5 \\]\n\\[ g(ax + b) = 2ax + 2b - 5 \\]\n\nWe are given that \\(g(f(x)) = 3x + 4\\), so we can equate the two expressions:\n\\[ 2ax + 2b - 5 = 3x + 4 \\]\n\nFor these two expressions to be equal for all \\(x\\), the coefficients of \\(x\\) and the constant terms must be equal. Therefore, we can set up the following system of equations:\n\\[ 2a = 3 \\]\n\\[ 2b - 5 = 4 \\]\n\nSolving the first equation for \\(a\\):\n\\[ 2a = 3 \\]\n\\[ a = \\frac{3}{2} \\]\n\nSolving the second equation for \\(b\\):\n\\[ 2b - 5 = 4 \\]\n\\[ 2b = 9 \\]\n\\[ b = \\frac{9}{2} \\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\(a + b\\):\n\\[ a + b = \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{9}{2} = \\frac{3 + 9}{2} = \\frac{12}{2} = 6 \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(a + b\\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{6} \\]",
