      "text": "To solve the problem, we start by substituting the given functions \\( f(x) = ax + b \\) and \\( g(x) = 2x - 5 \\) into the equation \\( g(f(x)) = 3x + 4 \\).\n\nFirst, we compute \\( g(f(x)) \\):\n\\[\ng(f(x)) = g(ax + b)\n\\]\nSince \\( g(x) = 2x - 5 \\), we substitute \\( ax + b \\) for \\( x \\) in \\( g(x) \\):\n\\[\ng(ax + b) = 2(ax + b) - 5 = 2ax + 2b - 5\n\\]\nWe are given that \\( g(f(x)) = 3x + 4 \\). Therefore, we can equate the expression we found to \\( 3x + 4 \\):\n\\[\n2ax + 2b - 5 = 3x + 4\n\\]\nFor the equation to hold for all \\( x \\), the coefficients of \\( x \\) and the constant terms on both sides must be equal. This gives us two equations:\n\\[\n2a = 3 \\quad \\text{(coefficient of } x\\text{)}\n\\]\n\\[\n2b - 5 = 4 \\quad \\text{(constant term)}\n\\]\nWe solve these equations one by one. From the first equation:\n\\[\n2a = 3 \\implies a = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\nFrom the second equation:\n\\[\n2b - 5 = 4 \\implies 2b = 9 \\implies b = \\frac{9}{2}\n\\]\nNow, we need to find \\( a + b \\):\n\\[\na + b = \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{9}{2} = \\frac{3 + 9}{2} = \\frac{12}{2} = 6\n\\]\nThus, the value of \\( a + b \\) is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
