      "text": "To solve for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) given that \\(g(f(x)) = 3x + 4\\), we start by expressing \\(g(f(x))\\) in terms of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[ f(x) = ax + b \\]\n\\[ g(x) = 2x - 5 \\]\n\nFirst, we find \\(g(f(x))\\):\n\\[ g(f(x)) = g(ax + b) \\]\nSince \\(g(x) = 2x - 5\\), we substitute \\(ax + b\\) for \\(x\\) in \\(g(x)\\):\n\\[ g(ax + b) = 2(ax + b) - 5 \\]\n\\[ g(ax + b) = 2ax + 2b - 5 \\]\n\nWe are given that \\(g(f(x)) = 3x + 4\\). Therefore, we can equate the two expressions:\n\\[ 2ax + 2b - 5 = 3x + 4 \\]\n\nFor the equation \\(2ax + 2b - 5 = 3x + 4\\) to hold for all \\(x\\), the coefficients of \\(x\\) and the constant terms on both sides must be equal. This gives us the system of equations:\n\\[ 2a = 3 \\]\n\\[ 2b - 5 = 4 \\]\n\nSolving the first equation for \\(a\\):\n\\[ 2a = 3 \\]\n\\[ a = \\frac{3}{2} \\]\n\nSolving the second equation for \\(b\\):\n\\[ 2b - 5 = 4 \\]\n\\[ 2b = 9 \\]\n\\[ b = \\frac{9}{2} \\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\(a + b\\):\n\\[ a + b = \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{9}{2} = \\frac{3 + 9}{2} = \\frac{12}{2} = 6 \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(a + b\\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{6} \\]",
